# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the Next.js 14.2.35 project with App Router.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 1.3.1 using npm
- Updated `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the new package and its dependencies (401 packages added)

### 2. Integration with App Router
Modified `src/app/layout.tsx`:
- Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next"`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component inside the `<ThemeProvider>` in the root layout
- Positioned the component after `{children}` and `<ThemeToggle />` within the `<body>` tag

## Implementation Details

Since this is a Next.js 14.2.35 project with App Router (version 13.5+), I used the recommended approach:
- Imported from `@vercel/speed-insights/next` (not the React variant)
- Added the component directly to the root layout without needing a client component wrapper
- No need for manual route passing as the Next.js integration handles this automatically

## Files Modified
1. `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
2. `package-lock.json` - Updated with new package dependencies
3. `src/app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights component and import

## Testing & Verification
✅ Build completed successfully with no errors
✅ Linter passed with no new errors introduced (pre-existing linter warnings in other files remain unchanged)
✅ TypeScript compilation completed without issues
✅ All changes follow existing code patterns and conventions

## Notes
- The SpeedInsights component will automatically track Core Web Vitals metrics when deployed to Vercel
- No additional configuration is required; the component works out-of-the-box
- The component is lightweight and won't impact page performance
- Metrics will be visible in the Vercel Analytics dashboard once deployed

## Environment Setup
- Created `.env.local` file with MONGODB_URI to enable successful builds (this was required by existing API routes)
